feat(kebab): parse and map the split dyld shared cache

The iOS 16 userland ships a multi-cache dyld shared cache: a 393 KiB main
cache that only carries the header, the 2,710 image records and the subcache
table, plus 42 subcaches holding all executable code and data.  dyld cannot
start from the old monolithic-cache assumptions, and nothing in the kernel
currently locates or maps the split layout.

Add a KEBAB-only module that reads the cache directory, validates the dyld
header (magic, formatVersion, cacheType, platform, the imagesOffset +
imagesCount*32 == imagesTextOffset identity, and the subCacheArray bound),
resolves all 42 subcaches by the UUID in their own headers, and builds the
VM mapping plan from each subcache's own mapping table.  The plan puts the
main cache at sharedRegionStart and each subcache at sharedRegionStart +
cacheVMOffset, keeps initProt/maxProt per mapping, counts the two .dylddata
and two .dyldlinkedit files as mandatory, and reports .symbols without
mapping it.

The default is a dry run: kebab_dsc=<dir> (or kebab_dsc=1) parses and logs
the plan at bsd_init and maps nothing.  kebab_dsc_map=1 is the second
switch.  Mapping uses vm_shared_region_map_file(), the primitive behind
shared_region_map_and_slide_2_np(), and therefore cannot run at bsd_init:
the kernel task has no shared region.  It is deferred to the first exec,
where vm_map_exec() has created one, and runs for pid 1 only.  If the region
is missing or already populated the module logs the exact errno and leaves
the cache unmapped; it never reports a mapping that did not happen.

Also add scripts/check-shared-cache.py, the host-side twin of the parser and
plan builder, so the kernel rules and the real 44 files can be compared
without hardware.  On the extracted iOS 16.7.16 cache it resolves 42/42
subcaches, produces 43 files / 51 mappings / 0xa2de4000 mapped bytes, and
leaves 0x4000 of slack at the top of the 0xaade8000-byte region.

Builds as DEVELOPMENT/KEBAB for arm64; no warnings from the new file.
